  "query_preview": "..\nDrier air with precipitable water (PWAT) values around 1.8 inches\ncontinue to sit over the region with drier air aloft, so shower\ncoverage remains isolated and is expected to continue through the\nnext several days. Light and variable winds may allow for island-\neffect showers before increasing to gentle to moderate out of the\nnortheast by the end of the work week. Ensemble model guidance \ncontinues to show a passage anywhere between south of Guam to north \nof Saipan as a tropical storm or typhoon. Uncertainty is still high \nregarding timing, location, and intensity, but the potential for \nheavy rainfall and gusty winds continues to increase as the weekend\napproaches. Residents of the Marianas can begin basic early\npreparedness tasks such as cleaning storm drains, taking inventory of\nstorm supplies, and trimming overgrown foliage as well as monitoring\nthe forecast through the week.",
